            <ArticleTitle>Primary squamous cell carcinoma of the stomach presenting as a huge retroperitoneal tumor: a case report.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>283-4</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.17235/reed.2015.3795/2015</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>A man complained of upper abdominal pain and early satiety for one month. An upper gastrointestinal endoscopy showed nothing specia. A CT scan of the abdomen was perfromed, which demonstrated a huge heterogeneous retroperitoneal mass close to the dorsal wall of the stomach and surrounding the abdominal aortic and celiac trunk. The resected specimen suggested that an un-regular tumor invaded to the dorsal wall of the stomach. Postoperative histological examination confirmed that it was a gastric squamous cell carcinoma.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
